{\b Chapter 5 Results}\par
\par
{\b 5.1 Introduction}\par
This chapter presents the results of the study examining how formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement influence student trust and perceived value of IT micro-credentials among Thai IT students. The chapter reports respondent characteristics, data screening procedures, randomization and manipulation checks, reliability and validity results, hypothesis testing, mediation analysis, and exploratory findings.\par
\par
{\b 5.2 Respondent Profile}\par
This section should present descriptive statistics for the final sample, including the number of valid respondents and key background characteristics such as level of study, university type, year of study, prior MOOC experience, prior micro-credential experience, and familiarity with the term micro-credential.\par
\par
Suggested tables:\par
- Table 5.1 Respondent Demographic Profile\par
- Table 5.2 Background Experience with MOOCs and Micro-Credentials\par
\par
{\b 5.3 Data Screening and Cleaning}\par
This section should report how the raw data were screened before the main analysis. It should state how many responses were removed due to ineligibility, incompleteness, failed attention checks, duplication, or implausibly short completion times. The final number of usable responses should be stated clearly.\par
\par
Suggested content:\par
- total responses received\par
- responses removed and reasons\par
- final usable sample size\par
\par
{\b 5.4 Randomization and Manipulation Checks}\par
This section should report whether respondents were reasonably balanced across the eight experimental conditions and whether the recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement manipulations were perceived as intended. The results of the manipulation-check items should be presented clearly.\par
\par
Suggested tables:\par
- Table 5.3 Distribution of Respondents Across Experimental Conditions\par
- Table 5.4 Randomization Check Results\par
- Table 5.5 Manipulation Check Results\par
\par
{\b 5.5 Reliability and Validity Results}\par
This section should present the reliability results for the student trust, perceived value, and exploratory enrollment intention scales. If any item revisions or exclusions were necessary, they should be explained. If exploratory factor analysis or related checks were conducted, they should be summarized briefly.\par
\par
Suggested tables:\par
- Table 5.6 Reliability Results for Multi-Item Scales\par
- Table 5.7 Item Descriptives and Item-Total Statistics\par
\par
{\b 5.6 Hypothesis Testing}\par
This section should report the main results for H1 to H10 in the same order that the hypotheses were presented in Chapter 3. Each hypothesis should be reported with a short interpretive paragraph followed by the relevant statistical result.\par
\par
Suggested subsections:\par
{\b 5.6.1 H1: Formal Recognition and Student Trust}\par
{\b 5.6.2 H2: Stackability and Student Trust}\par
{\b 5.6.3 H3: Industry Endorsement and Student Trust}\par
{\b 5.6.4 H4: Student Trust and Perceived Value}\par
{\b 5.6.5 H5: Formal Recognition and Perceived Value}\par
{\b 5.6.6 H6: Stackability and Perceived Value}\par
{\b 5.6.7 H7: Industry Endorsement and Perceived Value}\par
\par
Suggested tables:\par
- Table 5.8 Regression Results for Student Trust\par
- Table 5.9 Regression Results for Perceived Value\par
\par
{\b 5.7 Mediation Results}\par
This section should present the bootstrap mediation results for H8, H9, and H10. The chapter should report whether student trust significantly mediates the effects of formal recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement on perceived value.\par
\par
Suggested content:\par
- direct effects\par
- indirect effects\par
- confidence intervals\par
- interpretation of full or partial mediation\par
\par
Suggested table:\par
- Table 5.10 Bootstrap Mediation Results\par
\par
{\b 5.8 Exploratory Findings}\par
This section should report non-core analyses that remain useful for interpretation but do not replace the main hypothesis tests. These may include the relationship between perceived value and enrollment intention, subgroup comparisons between undergraduate and postgraduate students, and comparisons based on prior MOOC or micro-credential experience.\par
\par
Suggested table:\par
- Table 5.11 Exploratory Analysis Results\par
\par
{\b 5.9 Chapter Summary}\par
This section should briefly summarize the key empirical findings of the chapter. It should state which hypotheses were supported, which were not supported, and what the overall pattern of findings suggests. The section should close by linking the results chapter to Chapter 6, where the findings will be interpreted in relation to theory, practice, and policy.\par
}
